<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mpande</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1798–<a href="/years/1872">1872</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Mpande kaSenzangakhona</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mpande kaSenzangakhona was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> from 1840 to 1872, a half-brother of Shaka and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingane">Dingane</a>. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> support he overthrew Dingane in 1840 and went on to reign longer than any other Zulu king, keeping peace with the British in Natal at the cost of the land south of the Thukela. Late in his reign his sons fought for the succession, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetshwayo">Cetshwayo</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbuyazi">Mbuyazi</a> in 1856.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mpande.jpg" alt="Mpande" width="320" height="465" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King-Mpande-1.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mpande was born in Babanango, Zululand, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senzangakhona">Senzangakhona kaJama</a> and his ninth wife Songiya kaNgotsha Hlabisa. Considered weak beside his contemporaries and showing no interest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> power politics, he was allowed to live when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingane">Dingane</a> assassinated Shaka in 1828 and other half-brothers were eliminated. After Dingane's defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-blood-river">Battle of Blood River</a> in December 1838, Mpande defied him in September 1839, refusing to join a war against the Swazi, and led thousands of Zulus into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> republic of Natalia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andries-pretorius">Andries Pretorius</a> and Gert Rudolph backed him in the hope of concessions. In January 1840 his army under Nongalaza defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingane">Dingane</a> at the Battle of Maqongqo, and Mpande, arriving with Pretorius's force, was proclaimed king; the Boers were at once granted a large stretch of territory. In 1843 he had his brother Gqugqu killed, with his wives and children, for allegedly plotting against him, and many refugees fled into Natal. The same year the British commissioner Henry Cloete negotiated a treaty defining the borders of Natal and Zululand.</p><p>In the early 1850s Mpande raided the lands around his kingdom, culminating in the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eswatini">Swaziland</a> in 1852; according to the historian Philip Bonner, he wanted control of it because he feared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> expansion from Natal. British pressure made him withdraw. His eldest son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetshwayo">Cetshwayo</a>, who had led well in the campaign, came into conflict with the favourite son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbuyazi">Mbuyazi</a>, as Mpande declared neither mother his Great Wife, and Cetshwayo crushed Mbuyazi's followers at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ndondakusuka">Battle of Ndondakusuka</a>, massacring survivors, among them five of his brothers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>From then on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetshwayo">Cetshwayo</a> was the de facto ruler and Mpande kept only ceremonial functions; in 1861 Cetshwayo had Mpande's favourite wife Nomantshali and her children killed. Mpande died in late 1872, the exact date kept secret to secure a smooth succession. J. Y. Gibson called him the fool of the family, while James O. Gump describes him as a savvy survivor in the Machiavellian world of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> politics. He allowed John Colenso to codify Zulu grammar and translate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> into Zulu, and Colenso's associate Magema Fuze praised his peaceful rule.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mpande kaSenzangakhona” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Relations with the Zulu were the main threat: Zulu armies attacked the Swazi in the 1830s and again under <b>Mpande</b> in the 1840s, and Mswati sought support from the British in Natal and from Boer settlers at Lydenburg, to whom he ceded land in 1846 and 1855.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swazi-kingdom">Swazi Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His half-brother <b>Mpande</b>, with Boer support, overthrew him in 1840 and ruled for more than three decades, a period of relative stability in which the kingdom accepted the loss of land south of the Thukela to the British Colony of Natal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The throne passed by force more often than by rule: Dingane and Mhlangana murdered Shaka in 1828, <b>Mpande</b> overthrew Dingane with Voortrekker support, and Cetshwayo defeated his brother Mbuyazi at Ndondakusuka in 1856 and took over his father’s authority long before Mpande died in 1872.</blockquote><a href="/societies/zulu-kingdom">Social Hierarchy of the Zulu Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1840 the Boers helped <b>Mpande</b> overthrow Dingane and seized thousands of cattle from the Zulu Kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/natalia-republic">Natalia Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1854 Boer farmers obtained a strip of land along the Buffalo River on the Zulu border from King <b>Mpande</b> and founded the village of Utrecht, organising themselves as a small independent republic.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-utrecht">Republic of Utrecht</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Zululand District Municipality, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. Died in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-28.32,31.42&amp;year=1846" aria-label="Zululand District Municipality on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5372 3200 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mpande"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5822 3470h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5822 3470h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-28.32,31.42&amp;year=1846">What was here in 1846? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
